er on this issue  hi good afternoon anna thank you well  first of all maybe i would stress uh one  thing that  the eu does not recognize or  not recognize representatives or regimes  or  countries or governments or  administrations this is a competence of  individual  and each member state so this is on the  issue of recognition  we can recognize processes such as  elections for example in case of  venezuela  you know very well that we didn't  recognize the elections and their  results  um in december as uh free and democratic  so we are not recognizing  the procedures we don't recognize the  results and as a consequence  we don't recognize the institution which  was formed based on  this process but when it comes to  recognition of people and  representatives this is really  competence of the individual member  states on the declaration itself  this is text agreed and adopted by 27 eu  member states so the text is capturing  the position of the eu and the position  of the eu is saying that we consider  juan guaydo  and the members of the last  democratically elected institution which  is the outgoing  national assembly as particularly  relevant interlocutors  with whom we will continue working with  them in search of  a peaceful sustainable and democratic  solution to the venezuelan crisis  thank you peter are there other  questions  on venezuela  anna you have a follow-up  please but in the pa in the past in  other statements  uh guido was mentioned as president and  now  not anymore so there is a difference and  i would like to understand why this  this evolution  again i think the declaration is quite  clear  what it is dealing with and what it says  so  we believe that this declaration made  very clear our views on juan guido  standing once the mandate of the  national assembly elected in 2015  expired but again this was the text  agreed and adopted by all  27 member states so really not up to me  to go into interpretations  what's in the text is the position of  the european union maybe to add  and preempt any other follow-up  questions on this issue  uh the issue of venezuela will be  discussed at the forthcoming  foreign minister foreign affairs council  this month  so there we might you might have more  answers but at this stage  i would really stick to the text of the  declaration which was  again agreed and adopted by 27 member  states of the eu  thank you i think uh we can move on to  other foreign relations issues and i  pass the floor to  [Music]  [Music]  i now move to momsheel who has a  question on foreign affairs  i believe good afternoon uh my question  is uh related to russia  on uh 28 december at the end of last  year  the russian authorities for the first  time designated  individuals as foreign agents  five russian citizens were designated as  foreign aidans  does the european commission has a have  a comment  about this matter  peter  we seem to have  yes sorry can you now it should be  working right absolutely  okay thank you thank you mom chill um  over the  christmas period or over the holiday  period we issued already a statement on  the issue  so i would refer you to that statement  but i can very briefly recap that of  course we don't agree with this  legislation and we don't agree with the  overall process on this foreign agent's  law  and uh we are urging russia to to stick  to their obligations stemming from from  international commitments  but again i would really refer you to  the to the exact text of  the statement we issued on the issue  okay thank you very much peter um i see  no further foreign affairs related  questions for us today and so i come  back to  oliver oliver you have the floor  hi yes um well uh  what's up announced that from i think  the eighth  of um february onwards all  the data um the person's data of its  users will be transferred to facebook or  will be  well probably not physically transferred  will but will be  accessible to facebook so that includes  your phone number  your address your ip address and also  the contacts you have  that you use through whatsapp there's no  possibility for people to opt out so if  you want to keep using whatsapp you have  to accept  that facebook is using these data for  its commercial purposes  of which we are quite well aware so my  first um  question related to that is does the  commission  think that in light of this uh  move uh its authorization  of the takeover of whatsapp by facebook  uh was justified or that in light of  this  actually the statements and the  presentations by  facebook were probably not correct and  not honest  so that's the competition angle and then  the data safety question  we all use this and also you and the  commission use this in order to  communicate with us  and probably amongst you in light of  this move and also in light of how  facebook is known  to have um abused eu data protection  and breach data protection rules and so  forth do you still think it is  safe for the commission uh to use  whatsapp  from next month onwards in order to  communicate  internally and externally thank you  thank you very much your first question  is  uh certainly for ariana so i passed the  floor to her  good morning eric good morning oliver  and uh and  everyone i hope you can hear and see me  yes we can so i think so  okay so as you know in 2014 we have  cleared  the acquisition of whatsapp by by  facebook  we had of course communicated on it and  why we had cleared the transaction at  that time  uh all i can recall uh at this stage and  i promise that i will get back to you  uh if i have further information uh  later in the course of the day  is that in 2016 we have actually fined  facebook  for um some misleading statements  and not complete information in the  context of the merger procedure  and uh this statements are really  concerned  the technical possibility of  automatically matching  uh facebook and whatsapp's users  identities  which actually already existed in 2014  